---
/**
 * A thousand hills, in perspective.
 *
 * THE IDEA. Rwanda is the land of a thousand hills, and that is not decoration
 * here: gradient drives fuel burn and hills drive route times, so terrain is
 * the thing that actually shapes fleet work in this market. Nigeria's hero is
 * a radar sweeping a flat plane. Rwanda's is a landscape. That single
 * difference is what gives the two sibling sites separate personalities
 * without either leaving the brand.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS REAL DEPTH AND NOT A STACK OF PICTURES. Each ridgeline sits at
 * its own `translateZ` inside one `preserve-3d` world under a shared
 * `perspective`. The browser therefore projects them, which means two things a
 * faked parallax cannot give you: the layers are correctly foreshortened
 * relative to each other, and when the camera dollies forward on scroll the
 * near hills sweep past while the far ones barely move, with the ratio falling
 * out of the projection rather than out of tuned multipliers.
 *
 * THE COUNTER-SCALE. A layer pushed to `translateZ(-900px)` under a 1100px
 * perspective is drawn at 1100 / (1100 + 900) of its size. Left alone the far
 * hills would shrink to nothing, so each layer is scaled back up by the
 * inverse, `(P - z) / P`. The maths is in the CSS, derived from `--z`, so
 * moving a ridge means changing one number and nothing else.
 *
 * WHY IT IS NOT BANDS ACROSS THE PAGE. An earlier backdrop drew translucent
 * contour bands the full width of a white hero, behind the headline, and was
 * removed for exactly that reason. This one is anchored to the right, behind
 * the route, and is masked away before it reaches the text column. The
 * headline keeps a clean dark ground.
 *
 * COST. No WebGL, no 3D library, no images, no JavaScript of its own. It reads
 * the `--p` the hero already publishes, and animates transform and opacity
 * only.
 */

/* Ridgelines, far to near.
   `z` is the depth in CSS pixels, negative being further away. `tone` is the
   fill lightness: distant hills sit closer to the sky because haze lifts them,
   which is the cue that makes a flat silhouette read as distance.
   `d` is drawn in a 1200x400 box and tiles horizontally past the frame. */
const ridges = [
  { z: -430, fill: '#39424f', d: 'M0 300 C 90 232, 168 268, 258 236 S 430 170, 528 206 S 688 262, 790 226 S 950 168, 1060 204 S 1160 236, 1200 224 L 1200 400 L 0 400 Z' },
  { z: -315, fill: '#2d3540', d: 'M0 322 C 110 266, 206 300, 306 262 S 484 196, 588 238 S 742 294, 852 254 S 1024 196, 1124 232 S 1178 250, 1200 244 L 1200 400 L 0 400 Z' },
  { z: -215, fill: '#232932', d: 'M0 342 C 128 296, 232 330, 344 288 S 528 226, 638 268 S 796 322, 912 282 S 1078 228, 1172 262 L 1200 272 L 1200 400 L 0 400 Z' },
  { z: -130, fill: '#1a1f26', d: 'M0 360 C 146 322, 266 354, 386 312 S 584 252, 702 296 S 862 346, 986 306 S 1132 260, 1200 288 L 1200 400 L 0 400 Z' },
  { z: -50, fill: '#12161b', d: 'M0 378 C 162 348, 300 376, 430 338 S 640 282, 772 324 S 936 370, 1072 330 S 1164 300, 1200 310 L 1200 400 L 0 400 Z' },
] as const;
---

<div class="terrain" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="terrain-world">
    <!-- Dusk behind the hills. The only warm light in the scene, so the eye
         reads the far edge as a horizon rather than as the top of a shape. -->
    <span class="terrain-dusk"></span>

    {
      ridges.map((ridge, i) => (
        <div class="ridge" style={`--z:${ridge.z}; --fill:${ridge.fill}; --i:${i}`}>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 1200 400" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
            <path d={ridge.d} />
          </svg>
        </div>
      ))
    }

    <!-- Contour lines on the nearest slope. This is the motif the design
         system already calls Rwanda's substrate, now lying on a surface that
         has an actual angle rather than printed flat on the page. -->
    <div class="ridge ridge-contours" style="--z:-40; --tone:0">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1200 400" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
        <path d="M-20 352 C 170 322, 306 350, 436 312 S 646 256, 778 298 S 942 344, 1078 304 S 1170 274, 1220 284" />
        <path d="M-20 372 C 170 342, 306 370, 436 332 S 646 276, 778 318 S 942 364, 1078 324 S 1170 294, 1220 304" />
        <path d="M-20 392 C 170 362, 306 390, 436 352 S 646 296, 778 338 S 942 384, 1078 344 S 1170 314, 1220 324" />
      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* The frame. It is anchored to the rail but deliberately wider than it, so
     the landscape has room to be a landscape, and it is masked off before it
     reaches the reading column. */
  /* MOBILE FIRST, and deliberately so.

     There is no reading column beside the hills on a phone, so the landscape
     becomes the hero's own ground: full bleed, no horizontal mask, pulled back
     in opacity so the headline still wins. This is a different composition,
     not a shrunken desktop one, and it is why the terrain is parented to the
     section rather than to the rail. Inside the rail it inherited
     `display: none` below 64rem and simply did not exist on any phone. */
  .terrain {
    position: absolute;
    /* The FIRST SCREEN only.

       `inset-block: 0` stretched this over the whole journey section, which
       is about 2.6 screens tall because the report sequence needs that much
       scroll to be pinned through. The hills are anchored to the frame's
       floor, so that floor sat ~1700px below the fold and the landscape was
       never on screen at all. It is the hero's ground, so it gets the hero's
       height. */
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    inset-inline: -14vw;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* `--lens` is deliberately unitless so the counter-scale below can divide
       by it. That means it has to get its unit back HERE: `perspective: 1100`
       is invalid CSS and is dropped silently, which left the scene with no
       projection at all while every layer still applied its counter-scale.
       The ridges grew to twice the frame and the depth never existed. */
    perspective: calc(var(--lens) * 1px);
    perspective-origin: 50% 52%;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  /* The vertical softening, as paint rather than as a mask.

     Two mask layers plus `mask-composite: intersect` is the obvious way to
     write this and it does not work: the layers get unioned instead, so the
     full-width vertical gradient on its own revealed the hills right across
     the headline. That same bottom fade was also erasing the solid base of
     every ridge, which is why they read as stray curved lines rather than as
     landforms. Masking the 3D world itself is not an option either, since any
     mask forces `transform-style: flat` and collapses the depth. */
  .terrain::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      var(--color-ink-950) 0,
      rgb(8 9 11 / 0) 26%,
      rgb(8 9 11 / 0) 88%,
      var(--color-ink-950) 100%
    );
  }

  /* DESKTOP. The copy takes the left, so the landscape moves off to the right
     and is masked away before it reaches the text. The frame is 148vw wide
     and starts at -34vw, which puts viewport x=0 already 23% into it: the
     fade therefore has to begin past the halfway mark, or the hills climb
     over the headline, which is exactly why the previous backdrop on this
     site was removed. */
  @media (width >= 64rem) {
    .terrain {
      left: -34vw;
      right: -14vw;
      perspective-origin: 62% 46%;
      opacity: 1;
      mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0 50%, #000 76%, #000 100%);
    }
  }


  .terrain-world {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
    /* Resting camera. The driven state below dollies it forward. */
    transform: rotateX(1.5deg);
  }

  /* The horizon. It sits in the band of sky the crests leave open, and it is
     the only warm light in the scene, so the eye reads the far edge as a
     distance rather than as the top of a shape. */
  .terrain-dusk {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: -10%;
    top: 18%;
    height: 52%;
    transform: translateZ(-560px) scale(calc((var(--lens) + 560) / var(--lens)));
    background-image: radial-gradient(
      58% 100% at 62% 100%,
      rgb(243 93 1 / 0.55),
      rgb(243 93 1 / 0.12) 48%,
      transparent 72%
    );
  }

  /* One ridge. `--z` is the only per-layer number; the counter-scale that
     keeps it the right apparent size is derived from it. */
  .ridge {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: -8%;
    /* Pushed below the frame so each hill's base is off-screen and only its
       mass and crest are in view. Sitting flush at the floor left the whole
       silhouette visible, which reads as a shape on a page rather than as
       land continuing past the edge. */
    bottom: -8%;
    height: 58%;
    transform: translateZ(calc(var(--z) * 1px))
               scale(calc((var(--lens) - var(--z)) / var(--lens)));
    transform-origin: 50% 100%;
  }
  .ridge svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }
  /* Hills are lit from the horizon, so each one is a touch warmer at its crest
     than at its foot. `--tone` carries the haze: far hills sit lighter. */
  /* Atmospheric perspective, the right way round: the FAR hills are lighter
     because haze lifts them toward the sky, and the near ones fall away to
     almost black. An earlier pass mixed two near-blacks and produced no
     separation at all, so only the rim light showed and the silhouettes read
     as stray curved lines rather than as landforms. */
  .ridge path {
    fill: var(--fill);
    stroke: rgb(255 185 130 / 0.30);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .ridge-contours svg path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.10);
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 9;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* ---- Driven. The camera moves with the journey's own progress ----------
     `--p` is published by the hero and is the same number that drives the
     vehicle, so the landscape and the route can never fall out of step.
     Reduced motion keeps the composition and drops the movement. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(.hero-journey[data-journey-live]) .terrain-world {
      transform: translateZ(calc(var(--p, 0) * 300px))
                 rotateX(calc(1.5deg + var(--p, 0) * 3.5deg))
                 translateY(calc(var(--p, 0) * -2%));
    }
  }

  /* On a phone the rail is hidden, so the terrain becomes the hero's own
     ground instead: shallower, wider, and sitting under the copy rather than
     beside it. It is not switched off, it is recomposed. */
</style>
